\documentclass{article}
\newcommand{\pair}[2]{(#1, #3)}
\title{Off-Policy Evaluation with Adaptive Data Collection}
\author{Y. Hoshino}
\date{March 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Off-policy evaluation estimates the value of a candidate policy
using trajectories logged by a different behaviour policy.
We study how adaptively collected data changes the variance
of standard importance sampling estimators.

\section{Related Work}
Classical analyses assume a fixed logging policy,
which makes the importance weights independent across decisions.
Recent work relaxes this assumption for batched bandit feedback,
but the reinforcement learning setting remains less understood.

\section{Notation}
A trajectory is a sequence of state-action pairs.
The \pair{s}{a} visitation distribution under the behaviour policy
is assumed to overlap with that of the target policy,
otherwise no estimator can be consistent.
We write the horizon as $T$ and the discount factor as $\gamma$.

\section{Estimator}
The per-decision importance weighting estimator reweights each reward
by the ratio of the two visitation distributions.
Weights are clipped at a data-dependent threshold
to trade a small amount of bias for a large reduction in variance.
The clipping level is chosen by cross validation on the logged data.

\section{Adaptive Collection}
When the behaviour policy is allowed to adapt between batches,
later batches can concentrate on regions where the weights are unstable.
This adaptivity complicates the analysis
because the standard martingale arguments no longer apply directly.

\section{Variance Analysis}
We derive an upper bound on the estimator variance under batch adaptivity.
The bound depends on the worst-case weight ratio within each batch
rather than across the whole trajectory,
which is what makes adaptive collection attractive.

\section{Experiments}
On a suite of continuous control tasks the adaptive scheme
reduces the mean squared error of evaluation by a third
relative to a fixed behaviour policy with the same total budget.

\section{Conclusion}
Adaptive logging is a practical lever for off-policy evaluation,
and our clipping rule is a simple way to control the extra variance it introduces.
\end{document}

